Christian Conciliation Hybrid/Remote Work Resources Office of the Provost Baylor Round Table Ignite Great Colleges to Work For® Survey Human Resources News Updating and Maintaining Position Descriptions May 18, 2023 What is a Position Description? A Position Description is an important communication tool between managers and employees that define what is expected in their work performance. In addition, establishing a cadence for updating our position descriptions is critical to our individual and organizational success. The procedure of creating and updating position descriptions is critical for a number of processes, including performance management, promotions, supplemental pay reviews, recruiting & onboarding, internal equity, employee training & development, dispute resolution, department reorganizations, and compliance with federal and state regulation. Job Profiles vs Position Descriptions At Baylor, we utilize both Job Profiles and Position Descriptions to define an employee’s responsibilities. They both serve important and distinct functions, so it is important to understand the differences between these two terms: Job Profiles are job classifications that broadly define core job function and the minimum and preferred requirements for a position. Job profiles consist of groupings of positions that perform work of the same nature and that have the same minimum requirements. Each position is assigned a job profile, and this information is stored and updated in Ignite. Job profiles are maintained by Human Resources. Position Descriptions are unique to each individual position and can be customized to fit the needs of each department. Position descriptions define the major responsibilities, significant tasks, and expectations for the individual in their position. Each department is responsible for updating and housing their employee position descriptions. Managers can utilize the position description template as a resource for drafting and maintaining internal position descriptions. Vision for Success: We want to create win-win scenarios where employees are supported in their career goals and each department meets their organizational needs. Think of the position description process as an interactive and ongoing discussion between managers and employees that defines and inspires success. An effective process for updating position descriptions should lead to these results: EMPLOYEE Better understanding of \"what good looks like\" for each responsibility they undertake. Understand their comparative advantages and how they create value for Baylor. Better understand how they can develop and improve their skills Better understand how they fit into Baylor's goals and connect with the larger Baylor family. MANAGER/DEPARTMENT Better understanding of employee's comparative advantages and training needs. Aligns your expectations and employee's expectations for good performance. Creates a more effective starting point for setting and tracking employee performance. Proactive process in preparation for departmental changes and/or responding to issues. HUMAN RESOURCES HR can provide better strategic assistance if a department's position descriptions are up-to-date. Equity issues can be addressed more effectively. HR may better communicate what the department is looking for in candidates. To assist in compliance with labor laws. Best Practices for Creating and Updating Position Descriptions: To meet this vision, we recommend the following process for creating and maintaining your individualized position descriptions: Internalize and Communicate the Process: Mangers need to understand and communicate the importance of updating the position descriptions with their direct reports. Managers should be transparent about how this process aligns employee goals with departmental goals. Ideally, this is a very positive conversation where managers and employees align on “what success looks like” for the employee’s performance and career advancement. Timing/Establishing a Cadence: Managers and employees should meet at least once per year to review and update position descriptions. This discussion should occur alongside the goal-setting phase of our performance review process each summer. Once a finalized document is completed, the department will be responsible for storing the position description. We recommend utilizing Box (link) or any other secure, Baylor IT-approved, file sharing site. Update the position description as needed throughout the year: Gone are the days where each set of tasks an employee performs remains the same for their entire career. Changes in technology, the increase in remote work options, and the changing needs and expectations in the workforce enhances the need for defining good performance in the workplace. Some positions only need a description review discussion once-a-year. Others may need a review several times a year. Remember, it is an ongoing process; not a check-the-box exercise. This review may be initiated by the employee or manager. Partner with your HR Consultant if needed: Your department’s HR Consultant (HRC) is available to answer questions and provide guidance on this process. If a department manager is recommending a base pay change, supplemental pay, or other compensation-related inquiry, your HRC will always ask for an updated job description along with a list of changes to the role. Baylor Human Resources Putting People First. Clifton Robinson Tower | Suite 200 700 S University Parks Dr Waco, TX 76706 One Bear Place #97053 Waco, TX 76798 askhr@baylor.edu 254-710-2000 Ask HR About Jobs Disclosures Visit Policies Human Resources News About Careers & Opportunities Back Research & Learn Back Staff Job Descriptions whyBaylor Apply & Interview Back Faculty Application Process Staff Application Process Veteran Resources for Interviewing New Faculty and Staff Back Welcome & Orientation Back Living in Waco Steps for New Faculty Back New Faculty Orientation Steps for New Staff New Employee Forms Back Completing Your I-9 Back FAQs for Completing Your I-9 Employment Forms by State Transition & Exit Back Exiting Faculty Exiting Staff Contingent Workers (Auxiliaries) Other Campus Employment Back Summer Camp Employment Visa Sponsorship and Support Back Hiring and Support of International Scholars Next Steps for International Scholars Additional Resources Benefits & Advantages Back My Benefits Back Veteran Resources Baylor Wellbeing Enrollment Back Benefitfocus Information Benefitfocus Login 2026 Benefits Guide What's New for 2026 Retirement Back Investing in Your Retirement Back Baylor 457(b) Plan Baylor Retirement Plan - 403(b) Importance of Retirement Planning TIAA Updates - New Features Preparing for Your Retirement Back Retiree Insurance Details Back Health Reimbursement Account (HRA) Medicare Basic Information Retiring Process Checklist Enjoying Your Retirement Back My Retiree Benefits Technology Benefits for Retirees Retiree Tuition Remission Retiree Important Contact List Retiree Advantages Retiree Benefits Overview Compensation Back Compensation Philosophy and Resources Total Compensation Statement Fair Labor Standards Act (FLSA) Merit Insurance Offerings Back Medical Insurance Plans Back Flexible Spending Accounts (FSA) Health Savings Account (HSA) Livongo Prescription Drug Plan Dental Insurance Plan Vision Insurance Plan Employer Provided Insurances Retiree Insurance Details Continuation of Insurance Coverage Voluntary Supplemental Insurances Pet Insurance Employee Recognition Tuition Remission Time Off & Leave Policies Back Family and Medical Leave Act (FMLA) Maternity and Primary Caregiver Leave Paid Caregiver Leave Paid Parental Leave Timecards During University Closures Employee Caregiver & Family Resources Back Adoption Assistance Program Crisis Resources Back Mental Health First Aid Course - Testimonials Care.com Spousal Employment Assistance Educational Assistance Expectant & New Parents Progyny Surviving Spouse Benefits B